  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nIn the wake of the cold frontal passage, it will remain breezy\nthrough sunset with west to northwest winds gusting 25-35 mph,\nperhaps even a bit stronger across portions of the Capital\nRegion and Berkshires. Dewpoints will continue to drop into the\n30s, allowing RH values to fall to between 40 and 50 percent in\nmuch of the Hudson River Valley and lower elevations across the\nBerkshires and NW CT, with a few locally lower values possible\nacross portions of the mid Hudson Valley later this afternoon.\nPer prior collaboration with NYS/New England DEC's/DEP's and \nsurrounding WFO's, given the drying fuels across these areas \nwith little or no rainfall for nearly 2 weeks, a Special Weather\nStatement has been issued through this afternoon for an \nelevated risk of fire spread.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures will hold steady or slowly fall through\nthe remainder of this afternoon into/through the 50s, except 40s\nfor higher elevations across the southern Adirondacks and\nsouthern VT. Temps across portions of the mid Hudson Valley/NW\nCT have surged into the mid/upper 60s, but will gradually fall \nback through the lower 60s and upper 50s by late afternoon. A \nfew passing showers will remain possible across some higher \nterrain areas of the SW Adirondacks through sunset. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOutside of a few evening showers/sprinkles across portions of\nthe SW Adirondacks, generally dry conditions are expected\nthrough the weekend and into the middle of next week. Chances \nfor rain increase later next week ahead of an approaching \nfrontal system and upper level trough, however there remains \nuncertainty regarding whether upper level systems phase and \nresult in a widespread soaking rain, or the systems remain \nseparate, allowing for a more limited chance for showers.",
